> had a copy of Whitesmith's Weird C for the PDP11 (the C that didn't
> have printf())... I could go right back to my job in the early 1980s.
> Or maybe not.
>
> Have a lot of fun.

I remember WWC well. We used it a lot in the same era as you on RT-11,
RSX, VMS, and cross-compiled to microprocessors -- the last being the
main reason for choosing Whitesmiths at the time. I spent a lot of
time filling in their equivalent of SPRs.

The use of the weird runtime library without most of the functions in
K&R was never explained, although they eventually produced an ANSI-
compatible library. I can only assume they got stuck with an early
implementation pre-K&R. In some ways it was better; I particularly
like cpystr() which concatenates any number of strings and gave you a
pointer to the _end_ for your next append, and still use
implementations of this on less-wierd Cs.

A big problem was lack of source-language debugging, so you had to put
in loads of (not) printfs or work at assembler level. I wrote a
program to annotate the MACRO listing file with the C statements. But
to be fair, even DEC didn't have S-LD in the early days.

We bought their Pascal-to-C and BASIC-to-C converters and a BASIC
interpreter of which the last two turned out to be mutually
incompatible and unusable and got withdrawn without any compensation.

I have the distribution kits on 8" floppies, at least for RSX and
maybe RT-11/VMS. But obviously I don't have the right to distribute
it. Even if you showed me a copy of your license, one of its weird
conditions (subject in the UK to Manx law!) was that it expired in 15
years.

Chris


