>say, some more analog, stone-based technology?
>  
>
PDP-11 operating systems have been running since the 1970s.
There are also 2 hobby emulators, SIMH and Ersatz-11 (which
is also available in a commercial version), that run PDP-11
operating systems on current hardware technology.  In fact,
Ersatz-11 runs RT-11 at about 100 times the speed of a
PDP-11/93 for the CPU and probable around 200 times
the speed of a SCSI or ESDI drive on the Qbus.

So the proven reliability is already available - although I can
still run the same PDP-11/83 from the 1980s, but I find that
it is usually necessary to replace PC hardware every 5 to 10
years.

As for being around in Y3K, perhaps you missed the point.
Most PDP-11 operating systems will require some fixes to
handle dates correctly during the next few decades.  Only
RT-11, as far as I know, handles the date correctly until
December 31st, 2099.  So in less than 100 years, all of the
PDP-11 operating systems will require the date software to
be fixed.  Since fixing the date for Y2.1K seems rather a bit
short sighted, I suggested Y3K as the next target with probably
the ability to handle Y4K as well.

 From what I noticed in most cases, although RT-11 seems to
be as good an example as any, the Y2K changes could easily
have been made in the late 1980s in most cases and there would
not have been the Y2K panic.  In the case of MACRO-11 for
RT-11, that program was already Y2K compliant when released
in V05.06 in 1992, but with only two digits used for the year,
almost no one noticed.

After another 2000 years, I agree there does not seem to be much
point, although as I observe, the current Common Era (aka
Gregorian) calendar seems firmly in place with only very minor
revisions from the Julian Calendar.

Jerome Fine

